From: WinBoat
Subject: [PATCH] viogpu_umd: Direct3D 12 user-mode driver for viogpu

A WDDM Direct3D 12 user-mode driver (viogpu_d3d12.dll, Windows x64, and
viogpu_d3d12_x86.dll) for the viogpu3d kernel driver: the D3D12 DDI the Windows runtime calls, on
vkd3d-proton's implementation of the D3D12 API over Vulkan (Mesa's Venus
driver on the virtual GPU). Meson option enable_viogpu_umd.

- umd_ddi.cpp speaks the DDI (D3D12DDI_SUPPORTED_0082, and 0052 for older
  runtimes: the tables only grow and newer arguments extend older ones) with
  the Windows SDK's and WDK's headers. vkd3d-proton's objects are the same
  COM objects as the SDK's interfaces, so the driver calls them through
  those; umd_bridge.c creates the vkd3d-proton device (no surfaces) on the
  package's own Vulkan driver, loaded directly (viogpu_vk[_x86].dll next to
  it: the Vulkan loader would also load other drivers and layers into every
  D3D12 program, and Dozen, Vulkan on D3D12 from Microsoft's compatibility
  pack, deadlocks inside D3D12CreateDevice), and serializes root signatures.
  Without a usable vkd3d-proton device (no Venus, a host Vulkan short of
  D3D12's needs) the adapter has no Direct3D 12: DXGI_ERROR_UNSUPPORTED.
  Caps come from vkd3d-proton, kept to what the
  runtime insists on (IOCoherent on x86, view instancing needs SM 6.1, native
  16-bit ops SM 6.2, shader models up to 6.6 at DDI 0082, feature level 12_1
  until the DXR/mesh/VRS/sampler feedback entries are there: reporting 12_2
  without them fails device creation).
- umd_state.cpp: shaders arrive as the bare program (SM4/5 tokens or the DXIL
  program part) with nameless signatures; each gets a DXBC container with
  made-up semantics that match on both sides of every link (WBREG/WBATTR,
  SV_ names, tessellation factors remapped). Input layouts, blend,
  depth-stencil and rasterizer states, root signatures (a deserialized 1.1
  desc), pipeline states.
- umd_resource.cpp: heaps and resources (committed, heaps, placed, reserved;
  custom heap properties and allowed-flags converted), descriptor heaps and
  views (2D/array/multisample rebuilt from the resource), queries, command
  signatures, pipeline libraries that keep nothing (the runtime removes the
  device when creating one fails; a pipeline loaded from one is created from
  the description that comes with it). Descriptor handles, sizes and GPU
  addresses are vkd3d-proton's.
- umd_command.cpp: command pools (an allocator per list type), recorders,
  lists (all 3D table entries but Blt/Present and meta commands), queues with
  a kernel context each (the runtime signals and waits on it), fences.
  ExecuteCommandLists returns right away: two threads per queue pass the
  submission's place on the kernel context to vkd3d-proton (a monitored
  fence the context signals opens a vkd3d-proton fence the lists wait for)
  and its completion back (the context waits for a fence signaled once the
  lists ran, so the runtime's signals follow the work). Each submission
  therefore waits on the GPU for the one before (~100 us an empty one).
- Presents: swap chain buffers (committed textures on a "primary" heap) also
  get a kernel allocation, a virgl texture the viogpu3d kernel driver uploads
  before every use (a GDI surface), which DXGI shares with DWM; Present copies
  the image there (GPU copy to a readback buffer, CPU into the locked
  allocation) and hands it to the runtime's redirected flip (broadcasting to
  no other context: the queue's own one is refused). MakeResident forwards
  those allocations to the kernel.
- Entries not implemented are stubs that log their name
  (VIOGPU_D3D12_LOG=<file>), each with its entry's own type (so that x86's
  __stdcall pops the arguments); umd_tables.h comes from the WDK
  (gen_tables.py).

Through the Windows runtime: feature level 12_1, shader model 6.6 (6.2
before Venus from Mesa 26), double precision, 64-bit atomics, resource
binding tier 3; WinBoat's DirectX test D3D12 group 17/17 (queues, lists,
fences, root signatures, PSOs, triangle, compute, copy queues, bundles,
ExecuteIndirect, descriptor heaps) and presenting to a window, 18/18, in
64- and 32-bit programs.
---
